(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-11-04"] = {"6.1":{"s":"Oxidation and reduction always happen together, so the reactions are called redox reactions. In terms of electrons, oxidation is the loss of electrons and reduction is the gain of electrons. The substance that loses electrons is the reducing agent, because it reduces the other. The substance that gains electrons is the oxidising agent. Older definitions used the gain or loss of oxygen or hydrogen.","w":["OIL RIG: Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain of electrons.","The oxidising agent is the one that gets reduced; the reducing agent is the one that gets oxidised."]},"6.2":{"s":"The oxidation number is the charge an atom would have if all its bonds were treated as ionic. It follows simple rules: it is zero for an atom in an element, equal to the charge for a simple ion, +1 for hydrogen and −2 for oxygen in most compounds, and the numbers in a neutral compound add up to zero. A rise in oxidation number is oxidation, and a fall is reduction.","w":["Oxygen is −2 except in peroxides (−1, as in H₂O₂) and in OF₂; hydrogen is −1 in metal hydrides.","In an ion the oxidation numbers add up to the charge of the ion."]},"6.3":{"s":"A redox equation can be balanced by two methods. In the oxidation number method, find the atoms whose oxidation number changes, balance the total increase and decrease, and then balance the other atoms. In the half-reaction (ion-electron) method, split the reaction into an oxidation half and a reduction half, balance atoms and charges in each, equalise the electrons lost and gained, and add the halves.","w":["In acid solution balance oxygen with H₂O and then hydrogen with H⁺; in basic solution add OH⁻ at the end.","Electrons lost in the oxidation half must equal the electrons gained in the reduction half before adding."]},"6.4":{"s":"In a Daniell cell, zinc loses electrons at the anode and copper ions gain electrons at the cathode, and the electrons flow through the outer circuit. The potential of an electrode is its tendency to be reduced. It is measured against the standard hydrogen electrode, which is given 0.00 V. A more positive E° means a stronger oxidising agent, and a more negative E° means a stronger reducing agent.","f":[{"x":"E° = E°(reduction half) + E°(oxidation half)","n":"Reverse the sign of the table value for the half that is oxidised, then add. A positive sum means the reaction is spontaneous.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 6.4, p.90"}],"w":["Table values are for reduction. For the half that is oxidised, change the sign before adding.","A large negative E° means a strong reducing agent (lithium, potassium); a large positive E° a strong oxidising agent (fluorine)."]}};
